In article <Pine.A41.3.95.971023205536.26824B-100000@hal.cheme.cornell.edu>,
Lev D Gelb  <gelb@cheme.cornell.edu> wrote:
>
>The Alpha motherboards made before the LX164's  (Aspen called
>them "Durango") had either a 128bit or 256bit access path,
>depending on whether or not you filled all the SIMM slots.
>Maybe ten months ago, I remember some postings about not
>getting good performance out of it unless you filled all the slots.
>
>The new ones (LX's) seem to only have the 128bit option,
>e.g., fewer slots, gotta use bigger SDRAMS.   Does this incur
>a performance penalty, or is there some other factor at work?

There is another factor at work.  Apparently, the SDRAM memory
interface is much improved over the regular DRAM interface, so
that memory bandwidth is actually much better even with the
smaller datapath.

Have a look at:
http://www.cs.virginia.edu/stream/standard/Bandwidth.html

I abstract a few numbers from it here:

------------------------------------------------------------------
Machine ID                ncpus    COPY    SCALE      ADD    TRIAD
------------------------------------------------------------------

These guys use SDRAM:

DEC_433a-(0MB_L3)            1    292.6    292.6    323.4    341.3
DEC_164LX_533_2MBL3          1    241.5    234.4    267.4    256.0

This one uses DRAM:  (and probably 256bit datapath)

DEC_500-5-400                1    166.4    166.4    177.5    177.5

So you see that bandwidth is greatly improved, at least as measured
by STREAM.


PeterM



