Craig Burley (burley@tweedledumb.cygnus.com) wrote:
: lindahl@pbm.com (Greg Lindahl) writes:
: 
: > On an Alpha, the maximum swap partition is 1/2 gigabyte. However, this
: > doesn't cause the machine to fail, it just prints as it boots that the
: > swap partition is being truncated to 1/2 gig.
: 
: I hope you mean "*virtually* truncated", i.e. that only the first
: 512MB (or whatever) of it is actually used.  It's not, like,
: running fdisk, is it?  :)

That is correct.  OTOH on "out-of-a-box" Linux kernel one may have
up to eight swap partitions.  So, with a help of fdisk and editor
(to edit /etc/fstab) you may have back all your swap space again. :-)

It is not hard to hack kernel to make more than eight swap partitions
avaiable but even on Intel, with 128M limit per swap partition, the
need does not seem to arise very often.

It is also possible to swap on a file, although this is slower.
So, if you need some extra swap space on special occasions it may
be advisable to add a file to you swap then instead of blocking
permanently a big chunk of a disk which is not used nearly always.
A file for swapping has to be created in its full size (fill it
with zeros from /dev/zero) before sicking 'mkswap' on it.

I do not know without checking if a limit of 8 applies to all swap
areas only only to swap partitions.

  Michal




