In article <34471657.8C5820DC@scripps.edu>,
	"Jarrod A. Smith" <jsmith@scripps.edu> writes:

> Now, if the machine were laden with a lot of disk traffic (if you
> were a file server, were frequently accessing more than one disk at
> a time, routinely did gigantic file transfers in the background,
> etc.) then SCSI is definitely the way to go since the bus won't get
> saturated as quickly as with IDE.  But for single disk desktop
> workstations doing moderate tasks, it is probably overkill since you
> are mostly killed by disk access times on single disk machines, not
> the transfer speed.  SCSI really only shines when you are under a
> really high I/O load, and how many people truly fall into that
> category to justify the cost?  Not many I would guess.

After some recommendations from someone on this list, I looked into
solving my slow discs (quantum 3.8GB IDE) via scsi. You're right in
saying that access time is the limit for the vast majority, but you're
misled, as I was, in thinking that the *drive's* access time is the
deciding factor in a *disc system's* speed.

At work I now have a PC with a SCSI system in it with a disc that has
the same 8ms access time the previous IDE had, but the speed of the
disc system is far faster - NT boots *very* noticeably faster,
although I've never timed it. It's easy for anyone to notice the speed
difference - I've had comments from passers-by that the machine is
unusually quick. NT runs *far* faster on this machine now, as it makes
about as much use of the disc as unix. The difference isn't small, I
can't stress that enough! It's not due to disc fragmentation as NT was
installed on the IDE disc, played with for about a day, then
reinstalled on the scsi disc. 

The difference is apparently due to the operating system/driver
sending a list of requests for disc blocks to the scsi controller,
which then reorders them to require the least head movement, meaning
that the scsi disc's head would jump around less than the equivalent
IDE if both discs had the exact same data in the exact same physical
location on the disc surfaces. There may be other tricks involved, but
that seems to be the main one. I'm not sure that IDE *doesn't* do
something similar, but if it does, and I remember the main
cost-reducing factor in IDE was that the drives were very low
intelligence, it doesn't do it so well. I used to think that this
would be done by the OS, but EIDE killed this possibility off as the
surface and head count given to the OS is very different from the
physical count to get around some rather daft limitations somewhere
along the line.

When I can afford it, I'll be buying a scsi card and hard disc, it'll
cost me about twice the price of the equivalent (in storage space) IDE
system, but I'm fed up of waiting for my disc! I've got a P133 which,
for most tasks that take more than about 5 seconds, is I/O bound, so
upgrading my processor won't give me any useful speed increases.
Secondly I intend to get a scanner early next year and would like a
faster backup device, so I'll need some kind of interface card for
these, and I'll be running out of IRQs at the rate I'm going.
 
> I'm not anti-SCSI at all (or pro-IDE for that matter).  In fact, the
> thing on my wish-list is a BusLogic BT958.  But it seems to me you
> are slandering IDE without the facts to back it up.

Well, the difference I've seen on my work system pre and post scsi is
more than enough to convince me that the scsi evangelists are entirely
justified, whether my reasons above are correct or not. IDE is cheap
and cheerful, but scsi is for those who don't like to wait.

But why are all the scsi discs I've ever heard so damned noisy!

-- 
Ian Rawlings - ** decode anti-spam address to contact me **



