In article <62aof0$4g5$1@maxwell.EECS.Berkeley.EDU> peterm@maxwell.EECS.Berkeley.EDUNOSPAM (Peter Mardahl) writes:
>DEC CXX is NOT the same as G++ for linux/alpha, no, not at all, not
>remotely.

This is true.

>When I tried DEC CXX 5.0 and compared the resulting code to G++,
>the G++ code was 2x as fast, and compiled more quickly, with both
>compilers at their highest optimization levels.

Embarassing, but true for version of DEC C++ up to V5.2. The optimizer
backend of everything before that was, well, lacking. With V5.3 and
upwards, the Digital C++ compiler now uses the same GEM compiler
backend that the other languages use (and as those users that have
access to both DEC C and DEC Fortran can attest, these compilers can
beat GNU C and GNU Fortran quite handily), it helped DEC C++ quite a
bit as well.

>DEC CXX is a commercial product, while g++ is free, so the clear superiority
>of g++ wass shocking.  You'd think DEC would make a good C++ compiler.

We do now. The current version is V5.7 and should be good.

