In article <62338u$7ai@tardis.aris.net>,
Mubashir <mubashir@tardis.aris.net> wrote:
>epenneba@dynamo.cso.uiuc.edu (Erik Pennebaker) writes:
>
>>I've got a Multia 166MHz with 64M of ram running Red Hat 4.2.  gcc
>>is really slow, and the overall performace seems about like a 486 something.
>>Is this what I should be expecting out of the CPU?
>
>
>Same problem here.

This should be a FAQ for the newsgroup;
1) Multias have very limited memory bandwidth; as bad as a NeXT cube or
486 class machine on something like STREAM. Compilation is memory
intensive (though it certainly won't scale with STREAM).

2) Compounding this problem, the Alpha is a 64 bit CPU which doubles the
per-instruction memory load.

3) Compounding the previous two problems, Alpha is RISC; next to the
StrongARM, it is probably the most RISC of commonly used CPUs, hence it
will need rather more machine level instructions per bit of source code,
and correspondingly more time to optimize these instructions.

4) Considering the above, and other factors, compilation time is a
meaningless measure of CPU or system performance.

5) My Multia-166 is a dandy fast machine for the couple hundred bucks it
cost; comparable to a Sparc10 or P120 for the number crunching stuff I do
on it. If you want a real rocket, buy a 164 motherboard & stuff it with
SDRAM.

