Lupo -

The i960 was *extreamly* popular in the market it
was intended for, the communications market. All
of Cabletron's products are still based on it, a
good percentage of Bay's products are still based
on it, and there are many many other embedded
networking products that use that processor.

It went through a number of generations and got 
things like bigger cache, and different packaging,
etc.

The processor is now more or less "old", and
most newer designs are moving toward Motorola's
embedable PowerPC products (at least that's the
trend I'm seeing). The i960 just doesn't have
the horsepower anymore.

The i860, well, I'm not as familiar with that
beast. But I do know of a number of SMP machines
that were designed using it. I also know that
it was not designed to replace the 80x86 family
of processors, it was designed to enable SMP
boxes.

As for your question as to "How many machines
ended up using the i960/i860?". Lots. Lots and
lots. I personally worked on a project that 
shipped 2000 boards a year each with an i960
on it. That's one project, at one company.

Brian.
