Luca Cazzulo <luca.cazzulo@al.nettuno.it> writes:

> I am going to upgrade my P133 system on which I am running both Linux
> Slackware 3.2 and NT 4.0. I am not sure whether buying an Alpha based
> system or a Pentiun II/Pro system (perhaps dual processor). I am very
> attracted by Alpha, but on the other side I have heard rumors about
> Digital selling Alpha to Intel, so buying today an Alpha could not be a
> wise investment... Since for me this is quit an effort (in terms of
> money !) any help and hint will be greatly appreciated.

Don't worry about the rumors.  Not only might they not be true, even
if they are, it is *quite* unlikely that anything will happen to
"orphan" the Alpha line in the next 5-10 years, based on my guesses
as to the investments Digital has already made in the Alpha processor
line.  Worst scenario: Intel somehow buys the whole thing from Digital,
only to kill it off, which means they don't try to profit from the
64-bit architecture, existing system sales, FX!32 (which I'm guessing
is not exactly portable, that is, probably doesn't easily port to
compiling/interpreting x86 code for *other* CPUs), and so on.  Highly
unlikely, I would think.  I'd think it much more likely that any
Intel "intrusion" into Digital's product line would result in the
Alpha processor design being more "blessed" worldwide, but, if Intel's
bad rap is well-deserved in the future, Alpha processors produced
3-5 years from now might be "suspect".  Most likely of all, IMO, is
that Intel doesn't buy Alpha from Digital, i.e. things stay pretty
much as they are today, modulo whatever court battles and settlements
take place.

BTW, I finally gave up on trying to get a couple of donated/loaned
Multias working sufficiently (due to correctable-memory errors happening
too often anytime I put more than 32MB in them, plus some problems
getting Linux and related tools ported, combined with too-slow
performance for my One Dependable System [ODS ;-] needs when it
did work).  So, I bought myself a Pentium II 233MHz from Dell, which
is almost entirely ready to become my ODS.  (Once I get X properly
configured on it for 1024x768 or whatever, that is, plus get my current
ODS, a 486/33, on the same LAN with the Pentium so I can easily transfer
all my stuff over.)

However, the idea of the ODS is that I can safely do all my network
access, compiler development work, CD-R recording (archives/backups,
mainly), and so on there, knowing "all" the software will port to it,
and maybe, occasionally, even run Windows (gasp!) to play Asteroids or
something.  (Dell apparently doesn't ship machines without this stuff.)

Mainly, though, it'll be for doing g77 development under GNU/Linux, and
I hope to devote much of *that* time in the near future to improving
g77/gcc code generation for Alphas, which means the Multia(s) I
have kicking around *will* be useful, and if I can ever manage to
acquire a high-performance Alpha, I'd be *very* happy.

So, essentially, I got a Pentium II only because I ran out of time
and patience waiting for the Alpha(s) to show up and work right.
Besides, I spent my own $$ on it -- the Multias, and a possible
300MHz Alpha, are all donations/loaners for my g77 work, and it
didn't seem right to pester people to make these things happen as
fast as I needed to continue doing work I've already committed to
do (for which my 486 is really inadequate).

Also note that since I do mostly compiler development work, the
advantages of an Alpha in terms of simple performance are not as
clear-cut as for most other people's work.  I noticed that the Multia,
once I finally discovered a combination of SIMMs that worked with
64MB, still took roughly as much time to bootstrap-build g77 as
does my 486/33.  That kind of work is typical for me -- that is,
when I'm waiting for my machine, it's because it's compiling code,
either building g77/gcc or using it to compile code while running
test suites.  However, for people running, say, Fortran *programs*,
the performance advantages of Alpha running GNU/Linux are likely
to be higher than for compiler work like mine...and if you're using
g77, I suspect there'll be a greater performance improvement in
future versions of g77 (and maybe gcc) for users of a given Alpha
chip than users of a given Pentium chip.  (That is, we already *know*
about some pretty dumb things g77/gcc does when it comes to generating
code for Alphas, and have a good idea how to fix some of them
already.)

My medium-term plans are to get the Multia or possible 300MHz Alpha
up and running to become my main development machine in my office,
and move the Pentium II up to our loft with the synthesizers, making
it pretty much a "play" machine which I can also use, remotely,
to do Pentium-based g77 work.  Until an Alpha can become my ODS, I'll
use the Pentium.  I'll probably keep the Pentium as my net-access
machine, because such activity would probably interfere with
performance testing and benchmarking, and the Pentium is the machine
I'd care least about doing such work on -- Alphas are just so much
cooler to do compiler-optimization work for.  (Okay, they're not
VLIWs, but they're pretty neat.  ;-)

So, I'd suggest that you get a Pentium if you need one machine
you can rely on for "everything" because, at least in my experience
with two different Multias, the Alphas (and really the software)
are not there yet.  But, if you've already got a machine you're
comfortable with and are looking to get another, especially for
high-performance uses, the Alpha would seem to be a fine choice.
