Archer, Hogpilot wrote:
> >So basically i don't see any reason to go with SCSI on a desktop
> >computer.  The IDE is lower cost, easier to set up, and isn't that much
> >slower than SCSI.
> 
> Lower cost, yes, by a long shot.  Easier to set up, depends on who you
> are.  Slower than SCSI, yes, by a long shot, especially when you're
> talking about bus-mastering which IDE has never been able to handle quite
> right.  *You* might not see a reason for going with SCSI, but people with
> real work that needs to get done certainly see the need.  To each his/her
> own.

What specifically did you mean by IDE has never been able to handle
bus-mastering quite right?  Granted, not all IDE chipsets have bus-master
drivers or capabilites (neither do all SCSI interfaces), but the ones that do
seem to work just fine for me (I've tried the Triton and the VIA busmaster IDE
controllers).  I'm getting disk performance on my IDE system at home that is
comparable to my SCSI setup on my Sun at work, and with little to no CPU
overhead.  That's what it's supposed to do, isn't it?

Now, if the machine were laden with a lot of disk traffic (if you were a file
server, were frequently accessing more than one disk at a time, routinely did
gigantic file transfers in the background, etc.) then SCSI is definitely the way
to go since the bus won't get saturated as quickly as with IDE.  But for single
disk desktop workstations doing moderate tasks, it is probably overkill since
you are mostly killed by disk access times on single disk machines, not the
transfer speed.  SCSI really only shines when you are under a really high I/O
load, and how many people truly fall into that category to justify the cost? 
Not many I would guess.

I'm not anti-SCSI at all (or pro-IDE for that matter).  In fact, the thing on my
wish-list is a BusLogic BT958.  But it seems to me you are slandering IDE
without the facts to back it up.

--
Jarrod A. Smith   (jsmith@scripps.edu)
The Scripps Research Institute
http://www.scripps.edu/~jsmith



