: : Also, PIO isn't necessarily this high of an overhead -- I have gigabit
: : networking cards that use PIO. While it does consume the entire CPU,
: : it moves 20x as much data as a disk does. I'm guessing bug in the
: : Linux Alpha EIDE driver, but to confirm it would be nice to have
: : both EIDE and SCSI numbers for both Linux and Digital Unix on the
: : PC164.

: IDE running in PIO mode eats tons of cpu on every machine I've seen, so
: the fact this is true on an alpha is no surprise.

you simply haven't looked on any modern PC.  nearly every system
produced in the past 3 years has a busmastering EIDE controller
which uses around 5% CPU overhead for EIDE sustaining 10 MB/s.
this happens to be about the same as SCSI (810 controllers).
note that this is measured by how much it slows down a concurrent
cpu-bound process, _not_ by the long-known-to-be-a-joke CPU time 
accounting.

so if its true that a properly configured Alpha still burns lots of 
cycles doing busmastered EIDE, it's curious why.


: Also, IDE DMA modes are only supported for the Intel Trition chipsets (in
: the stock kernels, but I've seen patches for the VIA chipsets, and I've

no, there are half a dozen other drivers in 2.0, including the cmd640,
which appears to be the one used on 164 boards.  it does do busmastering
IDE, though I've only seen it on crippled machines, where it's not real fast.

