> hahn@neurocog.lrdc.pitt.edu (Mark Hahn) writes:
> 
> > I'll settle for "you've had a hard time buying any non-Triton system
> > for the past two years."
> 
> I'd settle for people to notice that we're also talking about Alpha systems,
> which do not use the Triton chipset.

Yes, that's an unfortunate decision by Digital. They could save a lot
on chipset and motherboard development costs if they made an Alpha
with a P55 pin-out. And we could save a lot on the whole machine, and
get a decent IDE controller, too.

Now I will get flames about the P55 only having a 64 bit wide memory
bus, and therefore abysmal bandwidth, but look at these numbers from
lmbench (1.1):

CPU Chipset Motherboard       Bcopy  Bcopy  Mem   Mem
                              (libc) (hand) read write
Pentium-133 Triton ASUS-TP4XE   41     37   71    83
21064A-300 ? Cabriolet          43     42   80    70
21164-366 ? EB164 128bit mem    52     51  103    72
21164A-600 ? 164LX             105    110  222   183

So, the Cabriolet performs very similar to the Triton motherboard, and
the EB164 with 128bit memory is hardly better. IMO the reason is that
while the Triton motherboard has a narrower bus, it has a higher bus
clock (66MHz vs. IIRC ~40), and uses pipelined burst memory instead of
the slower asynchronous variant used in (at least) the Cabriolet (I
don't know how much role the cache plays in this benchmark).

Filling up the EB164 (to provide 256bit access) would probably
help. Using a 164LX board helps a lot (even though it only uses 128bit
wide memory; again, higher bus clock and synchronous cache at work).

OK, the number chrunchers out there who need every bit of bandwidth
they can get will prefer the 164LX, but most of my applications run
from the cache, and for a good savings I would take an Alpha for a P55
motherboard (currently I take a Pentium), if its price/performance is
competitive.

For topping, here are some latency numbers (Triton numbers for L2 cache
corrected after looking at the graph; the other numbers look ok).

Chipset/Motherboard	L2      Main mem
Triton ASUS-TP4XE	65	   182
Cabriolet		60         368
EB164 128bit mem	L3:?       468
164LX			L3:?	   225

The guesses of lmbench for the L2/L3 latency on the Alphas under Linux
vary widely between runs, and the graphs are pretty unclear. The L2
latency on the Cabriolet was measured under Digital Unix. Does anybody
have any idea why the graphs look so clear under DU, and so fuzzy
and/or jagged under Linux (beyond the 64K-boundary, typically)? My
guess is that it has something to do with page or TLB allocation.

Followups to comp.os.linux.alpha.

- anton
