According to this article from www.sjmercury.com 
dec agreed to sell the entire alpha patent rights to intel.

Palmer in trouble at DEC


                    

                     Oct. 13, 1997

 BY CHRIS NOLAN
 Mercury News Staff Writer 


 ONE of the casualties of Digital Equipment Corp.'s failed lawsuit
against Intel
 might just be the man who launched the litigation, Digital Chairman
Robert B.
 Palmer.

 It's not quite like firing the coach after a losing season. But it's
close. DEC watchers
 have speculated since May that Palmer was in trouble for the usual
reasons -- not
 enough profits, too many problems. And now, less than six months after
alleging
 that Intel's Pentium processors were based on Digital's Alpha chips,
DEC is in the
 last stages of settling the litigation. The settlement will all but
take the company --
 which once dominated the computer business -- out of the chip
manufacturing
 game.

 As part of the settlement, Intel reportedly will pay DEC $100 million
to $200
 million for the patent rights to Alpha and fork over another $650
million for a
 semiconductor manufacturing plant in Massachusetts, where it will make
chips to
 sell back to DEC. Intel will also give Digital a discount on these
chips -- a move
 that is expected to transform DEC over time into an Intel-only shop.

 Palmer has been closely associated with Digital's chip manufacturing
business and
 the Alpha processors. ``If the chip business goes bye-bye, then Palmer
goes
 bye-bye,'' said Rob Enderle, an analyst who follows Digital for Giga
Information
 Group in San Jose. 

 Another sign that Palmer's in trouble: An engineer familiar with
Digital claims the
 move to get out of chip fabrication comes from the company's board of
directors.
 ``The decision to get out of the proprietary architecture business is
being dealt with
 at the board level,'' said the engineer. ``The board is now the CEO.
Palmer is
 therefore toast.''

 A DEC spokesman declined to comment on the speculation



