cbaker@wenet.net writes:

> According to this article from www.sjmercury.com 
> dec agreed to sell the entire alpha patent rights to intel.

While the article is interesting, for the time being, I'd
*strongly* suggest nobody make or change plans vis-a-vis Alpha,
Digital, Intel, etc. until they read an *official* Digital
(or, I suppose, Intel) press release.

I talked with a friend of mine who works at (and at times in the
role of official spokesman for) Digital.  I didn't press him for
"inside information" at all, mainly because I know it isn't his
job to give me any, but he was quite emphatic that only Digital's
press releases (which I haven't read myself, as I admitted to him
up front ;-) are to be trusted on such matters.  As far as whether
the "rumors" are right or wrong, he commented that, in essence,
it isn't worth telling the press either way: if they're right,
Digital would just issue a press release anyway, and if they're
wrong (say, if only one possible future scenario being discussed
in some meeting was "leaked" as "the" decision), there's no point
in telling them that, either, unless Digital is also prepared to
explain what *is* right.

BTW, wasn't it the same newspaper -- the San Jose Merrcury News --
that "revealed" that the 1980's inner-city crack epidemic was due
to a covert plan undertaken by Reagan and/or the CIA?  Consider
the source here.

Note that I'm not saying Digital hasn't decided to do what the
article says, or even published a press release.  But, trusting
the press, especially those elements of the press that have shown
a willingness to ignore facts and/or sanity in pursuit of a non-
journalistic agenda, to get these things "right" in the first place
is probably unwise, especially if you're about to make purchasing
decisions.

After all, for all we know, this is just a sneaky way for Digital to
boost short-term Alpha sales -- to those of us who'd want to rapidly
acquire "Intel-outside Alpha(R)" technology before it's too late.  It
occurs to me that, while Intel might pursue such a deal to keep Alphas
from multiplying, the effect of such a deal might be to keep future
Alphas from dividing instead.  :)
