References: <01bcc7b2$81428e20$621cc880@santorini> <slrn62eaqh.6f.miverson@stout.columbus.oh.us> <342884EE.53B9@roadrunner.com> <60tslf$17p$1@newsy.ifm.liu.se> <jaafgmkb0u.fsf@mars.gmdda>
Reply-To: mpc@genevaonline.com
Organization: M.P.C.
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha,comp.os.linux.hardware


I have found that there is nothing wrong with IDE drives on ANY desktop
system with one processor.  What is this crap about IDE drives not
working all the time?  LAMER!  SCSI drives and controllers are known for
being a challenge to set up, and from the time of the first AMI BIOS on
an intel motherboard you have been able to EASILY set up an IDE
controller and drive.  Not so with SCSI.

All of the new BIOSes that I have seen support SCSI booting.

If you need 7 GB of space, might i recommend an IDE setup with a card
(or most motherboards nowdays) that supports *4* IDE drives and use an
IDE cd-rom drive along with 2 (two) 3.x GB drives.  Ever had a BIIIG
drive take shit?  With 2 smaller drives it forces you to separate your
programs from your data files (well it does to me).

A desktop PC/Computer almost never takes advantage of SCSI devices.  IDE
drives:  CD-ROM, Hard drives, and Zip drives.  don't forget that you
will need to buy a more expensive cd-rom drive to go with that nice new
scuzzy setup.  Oh, maybe you can get your old IDE cd-rom and buy an
el-cheapo controller to run it.  YEAH RIGHT!!  I dare you to try!  And
cd-rom drives are soooooo slow that they can't take advantage of even
the ide bandwidth.

So basically i don't see any reason to go with SCSI on a desktop
computer.  The IDE is lower cost, easier to set up, and isn't that much
slower than SCSI.  

-- 
Andy Quaas



