>see the average student, including me, needing that much room.

well, i easily fill up >>4 GB of disk space with all of the CCD images
i've collected thru my graduate career in astronomy. i have a 2.1 GB
drive completely filled with 3072x300 (a little over 3 MB a piece)
long-slit spectra for just one of my projects. i hog a good portion of
a 9 GB drive at work with stuff from other projects. with the advent
of large CCD mosaics (8192x8192 or even larger at 16 bpp), too much
disk space is only marginally sufficient for us astronomers.

i considered upgrading and getting a jaz or syjet, but my experience
as well as that of others in the dept has been that jaz media is VERY
unreliable. just breathing on a cartridge wrong causes bad blocks to
form. i ended up putting my money towards a laptop so that i can at
least easily cart data back and forth between my home machine and the
readily available disk space and DATs at work. the extra 1.2 GB of
disk space didn't hurt, either. 

how much disk space one needs is highly application dependent,
though. i use about 1.5 GB for RH linux+applix+mathematica+a couple
large astronomy related software packages. therefore, if i didn't need
to have all that data around at once, i could pretty comfortably fit
into 2.5 GB. on the other hand, 4 GB drives are cheap enough now that
one might as well get one. better to err on the side of too much
rather than too little. plus in my experience, the current 4 and 6 GB
EIDE drives are pretty damn fast, much more so than the 2.5 GB drives
i've tried. i hdparm'ed a seagate 6.4 GB drive this weekend and it
rang in at 8.5 MB/sec. that's the best i've seen outside of a
barracuda or cheetah drive.

tim

