You didn't state your assmptions in your original posting -- I was
giving a counter example to your blanket statement about tagged
command queueing. It doesn't matter if intelligent arrays aren't common;
by not stating your assumption you might mislead someone.

> : If you have a system with one disk, EIDE works great.
> 
> too strong.  eide works great on multiple channels, and the truth is that
> unless you're using MD and striping, you won't be generating that much
> traffic on multiple disks.

Many chipsets don't let you seek on both EIDE channels at the same
time.  And the truth is that many Unix systems can generate traffic on
multiple disks without using MD or striping -- I see that all the time
in my systems, even if you never see it in yours. So most of all, you
should state your assumptions.

> people continue to buy hardware.  they need to know the tradeoffs:

If you aren't stating your assumptions, people don't know all the
trade-offs. 99.99% of the SCSI-vs-IDE postings claim one or the other
is better without explaining what kind of environment they're used in
-- single disk/single user machine, or multiple-disk/multiple-user
machine, etc etc.

-- g



