gryko@jsucc.jsu.edu wrote:

>    I called several Alpha vendors and it is not clear
>   if Linux/Alpha will work with IDE drives.
>
>   Some vendors explicitly told me that Linux/Alpha will not
>   work with IDE drive, other say that Linux/Alpha works
>   with IDE drive. So here is my question:
>
>   Does Linux/Alpha work with all or only selected IDE drives?
>   Do I really need to buy SCSI hard drive to run Linux?
>
>   I am looking for a fast and stable Alpha to run large Fortran
>   programs (molecular dynamics and DFT)

I assume that what you mean by IDE is acually EIDE. IDE is too slow even
for a Pentium. About EIDE, it's more a question of hardware compatibility.
If you have a EIDE PCI controller supported by Linux and by your hardware
it will work fine.

On the other hand, a SCSI is worth in this case. The performance bottleneck
in the Alphas is the disk I/O performance. EIDE and SCSI have more or less
the same I/O burst speed, but when you have multiple I/O request SCSI has a
queuing mechanism that makes it much much faster. To have multiple I/O is
the normal situation in multiuser systems (call them Unix or NT, but not
Windoz), it happens when you run a simulation in one window and compile on
another one. A slow disk access is like a Ferrari with bicycle tires.

At the same time a SCSI controller allows you to connect cheaper backup
media, like Jaz  or DAT drivers.

If your EIDE requirement is because of $$$$, you will save a lot more
buying a fast Pentium and you will get the same average performance than
with a poor configured Alpha.

Finally, a word about compilers. If you are running F77/F90 code consider
to buy a compiler that takes full advantage of the Alpha chip. There are
always many discussions about this issue in this group. Check
http://www.microway.com/ to get an idea of the prices and features of such
a compiler.

Linux on Alpha is great, but is not such a bargain as Linux on ix86.
Anyway, you save a lot of money compared with Digital Unix on Alpha.

--
Carlos Vidal
carlos@tarkus.se




