Organization: TU Wien, Institut fuer Computersprachen
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


In article <3434BCD6.D4504BDD@tarkus.se>, Carlos Vidal <carlos@tarkus.se> writes:
> On the other hand, a SCSI is worth in this case.

I'm getting sick of SCSI church missionaries.

> The performance bottleneck
> in the Alphas is the disk I/O performance.

That depends on the application, and on the amount of RAM.

> EIDE and SCSI have more or less
> the same I/O burst speed, but when you have multiple I/O request SCSI has a
> queuing mechanism that makes it much much faster. To have multiple I/O is
> the normal situation in multiuser systems (call them Unix or NT, but not
> Windoz), it happens when you run a simulation in one window and compile on
> another one.

Both my simulations and my compiles are CPU-bound, even on an
Alpha. If I have several processes to occupy the CPU with, faster I/O
will give me nothing. If you get into paging, you should buy more RAM,
not faster disks.

In any case, your multiple simultaneous I/O requests help only if your
simultaneous accesses are to several disks.

So, for a rational decision on SCSI and (E)IDE, here are some
facts:

- You can use both SCSI and IDE in the same box.

- The same devices are more expensive in the SCSI version (I call that
the SCSI church tax).

- Most of the faster disks are only available in SCSI version (and at
a premium).

- Some devices are only available for SCSI.

- Most motherboards include an IDE controller, some include a SCSI
controller.

- SCSI can handle multiple outstanding requests, IDE only one (per
channel). However, make sure that your SCSI controller and its driver
support this.

- SCSI allows 7 devices (plus controller) per string, Wide SCSI 15,
IDE 2 per channel (modern controllers typically have 2 channels).

So, my recommendation is:

If you have the choice (e.g., you don't need maximum-speed disks), put
one device on each IDE channel. For the rest, go SCSI. If you have two
devices that you hardly ever access at the same time, you can put them
on the same IDE channel.

Concerning the joker who suggested working one week at McDonalds to
afford the SCSI church tax, consider how much time you save with SCSI;
probably not a week, rather a few seconds.

WRT problems, I have seen enough SCSI troubles, and heard about some
IDE troubles. Basically, I'd say it's a toss-up. So isn't it worse
when you get both? Not really: The probability of a problem increases,
the severity decreases; you can do a lot more if you have at least one
disk running. E.g., if your system disk is IDE, it's not so bad if the
DAT drive locks up the SCSI bus.

> At the same time a SCSI controller allows you to connect cheaper backup
> media, like Jaz  or DAT drivers.

The Jaz drive is not a cheap backup medium, a (IDE) hard disk is
cheaper per MB (E.g., http://www.nre.at/EKGesamt.htm offers the 1GB
Jaz medium for ATS 1249 (without drive), and the 6.5GB SEAGATE
Medalist ST-36450 for ATS 4999).

AFAIK, DAT drives for IDE don't exist, so it makes no sense to say
that the SCSI version is cheaper.

- anton
