for daily backup, a DAT for long term backup (reading
from the last backup on the EIDE disk),  and a Jaz for troubleshooting.

The nice thing of Jaz is that it is removable, and the driver is light enough
to be pluged to any machine on my network (having a SCSI, of course).
In my network I have to maintain Linux-Alpha,  OpenVMS, NT and
Digital Unix (OSF1). With one Jaz drive and a few disk
I can boot any of these systems from the corresponding Jaz. disk. You
save a lot of time when you have troubles, is like a huge rescue diskette.
In my opinion, the cost of manpower should also count in the price per
megabyte, and in many cases a Jaz makes sense.

Once again, I don't think there is a universal solution in this industry.
People uses computers in so many ways that you can't follow any
"church".

